WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.602 --> 00:00:05.878
Welcome to our webcast, MBA Student
Insights, military veterans of the GSB.

00:00:05.878 --> 00:00:09.431
My name is Reeb Wilbin and
I'm a second year MBA student at the GSB.

00:00:09.431 --> 00:00:12.589
I'm joined by Valerie Rivera and
Jacob Sheehan.

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:16.164
Like me, they served in the military
before becoming a student in here.

00:00:16.164 --> 00:00:19.904
We'll be talking about the student
experience here on campus and

00:00:19.904 --> 00:00:22.221
answer some as many questions as we can.

00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:24.848
To submit a question to us about
what it's like to be a student,

00:00:24.848 --> 00:00:28.220
just click on the chat button at the top
of your screen, enter your question, and

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.980
click Send To All Panelists.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.840
We won't be talking about the admission
process, the application or

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.760
financial aid, but
staff from MBA admissions office will

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.900
answer those types of questions
directly in the chat box.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.220
So, let's begin with introductions.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.330
Valerie, Jakob.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.640
You want to tell us a little
bit about yourselves.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:50.920
My name is Valerie Rivera, and I joined
the Air Force right when I was 18.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.095
I enlisted as a Linguist and served for

00:00:53.095 --> 00:00:57.590
15 years and right when my enlistment
ended that's when I joined the GSB.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.610
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Jacob.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.740
Went to West Point class 2006,
and then active duty for

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.900
the last eight years,
Army Special Forces Officer.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.580
Deployed a lot of different places around
the world, and decided to come to the GSB.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.630
I'm actually still on active duty for
at least a few years after graduation.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:20.480
A teaching assignment at WestPoint,
so I'll be doing that for

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.890
three years after this.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:22.550
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.660
All right, as for me, I was an Airforce
Academy grad 2007, went from there to

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.790
the Kennedy school to do a Master in
Public Policy, and then as a guy,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:34.930
I ended up being a finance officer and did
some cost analysis on satellites in LA.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.300
Deployed for a year to the headquarters
[INAUDIBLE] working on strategy and

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.120
then went out to Korea for a year to do
a tour as the budget officer at Ozan.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.560
&gt;From there I decided to get out.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.780
I took a year off,
I spent some time with family,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.400
I traveled around, had a lot of fun, and

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.040
then decided that coming to the GSP was
a really good way to get back on track.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.190
And came here.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.560
And I'm going to go take a job with
a [INAUDIBLE] company in consulting

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.450
after I graduate.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.370
I start in July.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.050
So now let's get started with some
questions submitted by the audience.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.573
First one is how is the transition from
being in the military to being in school?

00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:19.958
&gt;&gt; All right, well,
I don't think it's as big of a shock as I,

00:02:19.958 --> 00:02:22.480
I guess, thought it would be.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.190
&gt;&gt; You're still in.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.005
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm still in.
Yeah.

00:02:24.005 --> 00:02:26.495
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think the biggest thing for

00:02:26.495 --> 00:02:31.965
me is, there is a very strong emphasis
here at GSB on social activities.

00:02:31.965 --> 00:02:36.715
And so, I think in the military there's
a lot of social stuff integrated

00:02:36.715 --> 00:02:40.715
throughout the work day, but there's a
very clear time at night where it's like,

00:02:40.715 --> 00:02:42.715
okay, it's six or
seven p.m., time to go home.

00:02:42.715 --> 00:02:47.960
And then everyone has their life there and
it all just blends together.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:48.650
The GSB.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:53.360
It's just one big academic,
social, and professional spectrum.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:54.830
The whole thing.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I guess for me it was a huge change.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.410
I actually did my degree over ten years.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.320
So there was night classes,
online classes and so

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.740
being at the GSB is actually my first
time really being on a college campus.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.020
So that's pretty much
a very intense change, and

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.900
I guess I thought because I was working
full time and doing school at night,

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.200
that it would be very easy to fit in all
the things that are here at the GSB,

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.500
but I found it fills up my day
quite A bit more than I expected.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I think here,

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:32.530
probably more unlike anywhere else
that I've ever been there are so

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.900
many opportunities of good
things to do in a day.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.260
It becomes a lot about being strategic
about time management and saying no.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.150
Which I thought I was good
at in the military but

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.450
you can really continue to
improve on that while your here.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.170
But it can be done.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:49.570
So if you're worried about the transition,
I'd say, dive in and

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.520
have faith that the skills you built
are going to carry you through.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.510
And it's going to be a big
learning process, but

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:56.570
it's worth it on the backside.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.310
So, number two.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.590
What are you learning about leadership
that you didn't all ready know from being

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.690
in the military?

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:12.170
&gt;&gt; So, I'm in a class right now with
Andy Ratcliffe called The Markets,

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:16.380
one of the things that he talks
about in the class is there's

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.230
leadership by example which I think we're
most familiar with with the military and

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:21.880
then there's leadership by design.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.150
And one of the interesting points
that he makes about it is,

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:28.280
a lot of people strive to
be leaders by example and

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.010
I think that's again something
that the military helps us master.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:37.330
The leadership by design is a little bit
more thoughtful in that the only way

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:41.160
you're going to allow an organization to
scale is if you can be a leader by design.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:46.230
Or you can design successful organizations
for other leaders to succeed in.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.280
And, leadership by example, I think,
we probably all burned ourselves out

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.220
a lot in the military, just trying to
be really good leaders by example.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.840
So that's the biggest learning I've taken
away from here at the G S B with that.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.670
&gt;&gt; I think for me there's more of
an emphasis on the diversity of different

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.450
kinds of leadership for
the different kinds of organizations.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:08.020
In a lot of ways it didn't feel
like I necessarily fit the mold of

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.190
a military leader but

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:14.120
here I feel like I have more opportunities
to explore what makes me really unique and

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:18.610
what I really bring to the table
without ruffling as many feathers.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.680
So that's been really nice.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.200
&gt;&gt; Excellent, thank you guys.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.800
Number three,
what are your career plans and

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.520
how do you think how your
post-military background helped or

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.890
hurt you in job interviews for
summer, or post MBA jobs.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.180
So for me, I can take this,

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:41.130
I'd say that my career plans have
changed significantly since I came here.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:46.350
And, I originally thought that I wanted to
figure out another way to address problems

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:50.000
associated with terrorism by
creating economic opportunities for

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.610
people in developing markets.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:56.430
And I think that's a big thesis and
a big thing to bite off and chew.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:01.090
But I think what happened is that I'm
evolving, so I'm going to go into

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:04.570
consulting for a couple years and continue
to build out a robust skill set and

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:09.430
then probably and
potentially stay there for a long time,

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:13.030
but maybe transition into another
career after I've built those skills.

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.780
And so I like to look at it in
terms of building blocks and

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:18.580
sort of three to five year plans.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:20.930
And in terms of my
post-military background,

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:24.090
I think the military is
getting a stronger brand.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.780
Where people like the discipline and
people like the fact that we are just

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:30.380
willing to sit and get things done and
not complain about it, and

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:35.270
so when you learn to translate the things
you've done and the skills you've built,

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:36.600
and put it in the right language.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.530
That was the hardest thing to transition,
but

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.190
when you figure how to put it in the right
language, I think people are responsive

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:44.210
and they're really receptive
to the idea of hiring vets.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.015
&gt;&gt; On a related note,
I'm not looking for a job yet.

00:06:49.015 --> 00:06:53.970
[LAUGH] But in the different groups
I've worked with, a lot of my classmates

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.820
have remarked how working with
veterans has been so wonderful.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.110
We start on time, we end on time,
we get the job done and

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.380
they really appreciate that.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.630
So I'm glad to help
bring that to the table.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:09.360
But also, as far as career plans,
I'm taking some of my frustration with

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.740
the bureaucracy and dovetailing on
what Jacob said, helping organizations

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.370
that struggle, build the kind of
environment where people really thrive.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:23.250
So I'm using my past experience with
the government and military and

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.910
then taking what I'm learning here,

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:30.570
to gain the confidence to
start my own consulting firm.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:35.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the first day of class,
I showed up 15 minutes early and

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:39.630
I was the only one in our 70 person or
70 seats in the room.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:43.380
And eventually five minutes before
class the professor walked in and

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.140
said, what are you doing here?

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:46.994
How come you're not outside socializing?

00:07:46.994 --> 00:07:49.944
I'm like, but I thought class started,
and he's like, but

00:07:49.944 --> 00:07:52.789
most people just show up a few
minutes after the class starts, so

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:54.914
even the professor sort
of acknowledged that.

00:07:54.914 --> 00:07:58.738
So it was an awkward start from that
standpoint, but for career plans, I'm

00:07:58.738 --> 00:08:02.587
still on active duty So [COUGH] I'll be
going to back to teach for a little bit.

00:08:02.587 --> 00:08:07.758
I think I did do an internship over
the summer and what I thought was

00:08:07.758 --> 00:08:13.023
interesting working for a tech start
up was there's a lot of things

00:08:13.023 --> 00:08:18.099
that in the private sector there
are habits that people learn and

00:08:18.099 --> 00:08:22.070
military candidates do
not have to unlearn them.

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:28.070
So there can be some bad habits from
previous organizations and I think that we

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:32.900
come in with a completely open mind having
transitioned from one organization to

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.720
say the private sector or a different
organization, so that's an advantage.

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:41.420
&gt;&gt; What do you find most rewarding
about your Stanford experience?

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:45.192
&gt;&gt; I think by far, it's the opportunities.

00:08:45.192 --> 00:08:50.090
I feel that we've won this platinum
ticket to see things that we've seen,

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:53.680
get the access to different people,
organizations.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.260
Speakers that come here
are just incredible, and

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:01.750
stuff that you would have to look
forward to like, in six months,

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:05.920
I'm going to see something really cool, I
feel like that's happening every day here.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.580
And the hard part is just trying
to decide what to do every day.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:12.965
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:09:12.965 --> 00:09:15.800
&gt;&gt; For
me on that same note has been the people.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:19.970
I am surrounded by consistently the most
talented people I have ever been around in

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:21.240
my life and career.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:25.345
And I feel like I've seen a lot of
different organizational contexts and

00:09:25.345 --> 00:09:28.439
that is both pressure,
because you're surrounded by

00:09:28.439 --> 00:09:32.357
people who are making you feel like
you're not very smart every day.

00:09:32.357 --> 00:09:36.005
But it's also, it's changed my life in
many ways and it has really upped the bar

00:09:36.005 --> 00:09:39.390
for me in terms of what I expect of
myself and my ability to perform.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:44.668
So that has been incredibly rewarding,
and people care about each other and

00:09:44.668 --> 00:09:49.632
there's a nice community that I
think that's a huge understatement.

00:09:49.632 --> 00:09:56.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think the ability to have some
time to experiment is really valuable.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:10:00.149
I think in the military, we get
really focussed on just performing on

00:10:00.149 --> 00:10:02.598
the function that we have and
the task at hand is.

00:10:02.598 --> 00:10:06.240
The stakes are usually particularly high,
particularly if you're deploying a lot.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.810
So I think having some time for
reflection and experimentation and

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:13.270
to really figure out what it is
that you're uniquely good at.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.861
And thinking about some of the
opportunities that Valerie talked about to

00:10:15.861 --> 00:10:17.416
be able to apply those
skills in the future.

00:10:17.416 --> 00:10:20.559
&gt;&gt; Nice, and I think there's
a lot of questions rolling in, so

00:10:20.559 --> 00:10:25.430
I'm going to try and have just one answer
from here on out but this has been good.

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:28.080
So are there any active
duty students at the GSP?

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.560
Also how is your experience different from
military vets and peers at other schools?

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.898
So we've got Jake.
&gt;&gt; All right, I got this one.

00:10:32.898 --> 00:10:34.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, so

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:37.983
there are two of us in the class of 2016.

00:10:37.983 --> 00:10:40.999
I don't they're in the classes 2017.

00:10:40.999 --> 00:10:41.926
&gt;&gt; I don't think so.

00:10:41.926 --> 00:10:45.970
&gt;&gt; Okay, and
as fas as experience being different,

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:51.172
I just know specifically at
Stanford that I've heard differs,

00:10:51.172 --> 00:10:58.030
and I've experience the differsm is the
amount of collaboration that goes on here.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.390
And that's just part of
the Silicon Valley ecosystem, but

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.840
it's pretty unique to
the academic experience here.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.640
So I think that's a really
large benefit for

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:11.520
an active duty student to come here and
have these two years be focused on

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.740
collaboration and reflection instead
of maybe mentality that is just,

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:19.000
I need to get ahead during
my time here for two years.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:22.800
So I think it's a great place to think
about how you can apply your skills to

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.200
the military afterwards.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.121
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:11:25.121 --> 00:11:28.618
&gt;&gt; Valerie, I am relieved to see
a prior enlisted graduate student,

00:11:28.618 --> 00:11:30.690
thank you very much for being here.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:34.891
As a prior enlisted member, were there any
unique challenges you had to overcome as

00:11:34.891 --> 00:11:38.927
a graduate student that perhaps prior or
current officers were not faced with?

00:11:38.927 --> 00:11:41.456
&gt;&gt; I think number one
is just the background,

00:11:41.456 --> 00:11:44.970
not having that consolidated
four year kind of degree, and

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:49.340
I guess a lot of my peers that are
officers have gone to service academies.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:53.700
So there's that bond that I
didn't necessarily experience.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.309
Also, I'd say that there are fewer
enlisted service members.

00:11:56.309 --> 00:12:01.769
There are two in the class of 2017, and
I'm just really proud to see us there,

00:12:01.769 --> 00:12:05.504
and I would love to see more
prior enlisted people here.

00:12:05.504 --> 00:12:08.729
I think sometimes there's a perception
that you're blue collar, or

00:12:08.729 --> 00:12:11.670
you can't hack it, and
I don't think that's true.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:14.150
I think if you work hard,
and you have this dream, and

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:17.820
you can really sell it, and
get yourself here, then go for it.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:23.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'd say, one of the more
successful guys in our class,

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.290
Andy Turner is a prior enlisted Marine.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:31.472
And he's done phenomenally well as
has Valerie, so you have the ring.

00:12:31.472 --> 00:12:33.492
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:12:33.492 --> 00:12:37.602
&gt;&gt; Okay, what are you studying or
specializing in?

00:12:37.602 --> 00:12:41.608
&gt;&gt; So we have essentially
a generalist track here as

00:12:41.608 --> 00:12:45.427
opposed to having a more
specialized degree.

00:12:45.427 --> 00:12:48.688
And so with that there's a lot of
flexibility in the second year to sort of

00:12:48.688 --> 00:12:51.010
take whatever it is that interests you.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:55.350
So in the first year there's a pretty
strong course and requirements and

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:56.940
you can take a couple of electives.

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:01.760
So that's fairly set, because they
want to have you come out of the MBAs.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.070
They want you have a standardized
product to a certain degree.

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:08.920
Just to know that you got the skills
you need to be versatile but

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.920
beyond that,
they've got a lot of offerings.

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:14.940
I think probably the strongest curriculum
here is centered around entrepreneurship

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.930
just because of the environment
we're living in.

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:19.950
But there's also really
strong financial skills,

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:23.280
there's a financial modeling class we took
that I think was pretty foundational.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:25.239
And then there's a lot of leadership and

00:13:25.239 --> 00:13:27.610
rolep laying that I've
been focused on as well.

00:13:30.884 --> 00:13:34.870
But there's lots of information on the
website about classes that are out there.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.833
Who did you have write your references?

00:13:37.833 --> 00:13:42.517
&gt;&gt; I can talk about this, so I think a
good thing to consider is someone who can

00:13:42.517 --> 00:13:45.600
write about you at a snapshot in time.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.940
And then so that was my company commander
when I was in Iraq on a combat deployment.

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:56.215
And then I had a mentor who had sort of
seen the larger arc of my experiences.

00:13:56.215 --> 00:14:00.476
So be able to zoom out and contextualize a
lot of different experiences that I had in

00:14:00.476 --> 00:14:04.460
different jobs and sort of show how
that effected my personal involvement.

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:12.530
So I think references should be written
by people who know you incredibly well.

00:14:12.530 --> 00:14:17.529
And can talk about why you're wired
a certain way or who as we do,

00:14:17.529 --> 00:14:20.685
rather than based on title or position.

00:14:20.685 --> 00:14:24.650
&gt;&gt; Yeah, are there GSB students
who are in the Reserves?

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:28.630
How easy is it to balance Reserve
component obligations with GSB academics

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:29.780
and extracurriculars?

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:31.640
&gt;&gt; So I can take this one.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:36.080
I'm still learning the ropes, but
what I've been able to do is enroll in

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.260
the IMA program through
the Air Force Reserves.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:41.343
And that allows me to put my
reserve duty in the summer,

00:14:41.343 --> 00:14:43.162
there's a trade off with that.

00:14:43.162 --> 00:14:47.335
I can't do an internship for example,
but I've been able to work with

00:14:47.335 --> 00:14:50.375
a great supervisor who's
found a way to get a job for

00:14:50.375 --> 00:14:55.220
me over the summer that's uniquely in line
with my strengths and what I want to do.

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:57.010
So that's pretty exciting,

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:01.760
the other thing is that there is
a really interesting unit in the local

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:06.500
area called a defense innovation unit
experimental down at market fields.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:11.430
And so that may be an option for
people looking to come to school here too.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:15.534
&gt;&gt; Excellent, let's see besides
the bets club what other social and

00:15:15.534 --> 00:15:18.580
professional clubs are you involved with?

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:20.269
Can you talk about your
experience with the vets club?

00:15:24.066 --> 00:15:29.770
Yeah, so I think there's no shortage
of extracurriculars around here.

00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:30.800
Like we said before,

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:36.845
it's really a monumental task trying
to figure out what to say no to.

00:15:36.845 --> 00:15:41.723
So there's the energy club I've
done a little bit of time with.

00:15:41.723 --> 00:15:45.800
We just stood up a virtual reality club.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:48.647
I think there's 38 student clubs, so

00:15:48.647 --> 00:15:53.264
all sorts of either diversity clubs or
any flavors, you name it.

00:15:53.264 --> 00:15:55.724
There's an entrepreneurship club,
venture capital, finance.

00:15:55.724 --> 00:15:57.605
There will be a club centered around.

00:15:57.605 --> 00:16:02.486
The industry that you're interested in
going to, and if not you could make one.

00:16:02.486 --> 00:16:08.090
And there's always a group of interested
students that will join you in that.

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:09.900
And that provides an amazing platform,

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:13.360
you can leverage to bring in cutting
edge speakers in those fields.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.290
So I'm very happy with those.

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:19.470
And specifically with our experience with
the Best Club, I think that the Best Club

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:23.800
is growing in terms of its
platform here with the GSB.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.440
When I got here it was
kind of a low key thing.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:26.530
There was a little bit of a social.

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:28.120
There wasn't a lot of program around it.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.110
I think a lot of us,
it's a smaller community.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:31.780
There's probably 20
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:33.310
&gt;&gt; that's between the two classes roughly.

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:37.620
And so people were kind of
getting here and going out and

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:41.740
exploring a lot of other things versus
spending a lot of time with other vets.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:45.450
But I think the class of 2017
is really upping the game there.

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:46.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:50.790
we had a military service banquet
this year that had 300 people.

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:54.150
I was shocked because it's bigger than
some of the military events I've been in

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:55.340
when I was active duty.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:59.750
So, it was great to see not
just military and veterans, but

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:01.120
also our class mates attended.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:02.510
And I thought that was really wonderful.

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:03.200
&gt;&gt; It was really great.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:06.930
&gt;&gt; They were just really curious
about us and our heritage.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:09.170
Everybody wanted to see us in uniform too.

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:10.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:11.290
&gt;&gt; So that was very interesting.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:16.000
&gt;&gt; How certain were you on the career
you wanted to pursue prior to

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:16.900
starting your MBA?

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:20.570
I ask because I'm wondering how
essential your internship is to getting

00:17:20.570 --> 00:17:22.290
the job you want upon graduation.

00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:28.910
So, this probably applies for
me since I've had an internship.

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:35.330
So, a lot of people come here,
do their internship, come back from their

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:39.820
second year and realize they didn't like
their internship at all and there's a lot

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:44.060
of our classmates right now that are two
weeks out and are still looking for jobs.

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:45.530
And it's not because
there's not jobs available,

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:47.130
it's because they're really
trying to find the right fit.

00:17:48.395 --> 00:17:52.510
And, I had a thesis about
what my career would be and

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:57.000
I think it's important to have a thesis
and to build that together and

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.770
make that show through
in your application.

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:02.770
But I think that what they're looking for
in the application process,

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.780
we're not going to talk about
that too much specifically,

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:08.140
I think they want it to show that you
can build a vision that's coherent, but

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:09.930
there's also an understanding
that you're going to get here and

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.640
you're going to meet all
the new kinds of people.

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:13.400
And that's going to change and
that's okay.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:16.390
So I think it's important to
have vision and build that.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.276
But you don't need to be anchored to it.

00:18:21.502 --> 00:18:23.159
Do vets go the entrepreneurial route?

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:27.880
Well.
&gt;&gt; I think so, I mean that's my plan and

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:32.690
for me it's not about having that freedom
that I was craving where I was in

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:37.730
to make my own choices about what to do,
where to live, who to work with,

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:38.490
all of those things.

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:43.440
So I'm excited about the process and
I'm excited about testing it out while I'm

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:47.070
here and I think that's going to
give me even more confidence,

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:52.300
that yes this is the path and
just step out as soon as I graduate.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:57.930
&gt;&gt; So there's a popular class here at
the GSD called entrepreneurial acquisition

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:02.445
NIC See a lot of vets interested
in the entrepreneurial route

00:19:02.445 --> 00:19:06.185
where they work with investors to acquire
a small business and then they grow it.

00:19:06.185 --> 00:19:10.275
There were a lot of veterans who did that
last year as a percentage of how many

00:19:10.275 --> 00:19:11.325
vets are here.

00:19:11.325 --> 00:19:15.365
And I just think in general we're
comfortable with risk-taking,

00:19:15.365 --> 00:19:19.025
particularly from a lot of our deployment
experiences and we're looking to do so

00:19:19.025 --> 00:19:22.560
in a way that's not part
of a larger bureaucracy.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:25.090
And so I think a lot of us come
here with something to prove and

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:26.850
want to test themselves as entrepreneurs,

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:29.020
and so I think that's why
it's a good fit to come here.

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:29.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:33.110
And one notable GSB alum,
I forget his name,

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:37.390
but founded TRX coming out of here and
now you see them at bases everywhere.

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:42.170
So you guys, if you use TRX bands,
that was a GSB incubated experience, so.

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:45.210
During what class, group, or

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:48.510
experience have you most heavily relied
upon your skills acquired in the military?

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:53.130
&gt;&gt; I think this is a tough one.

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:54.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:54.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:20:02.170
&gt;&gt; Well I think that spending
a lot of time overseas,

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:06.810
working on negotiation is I think
a very popular class here and

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:10.840
personal dynamics known more
colloquially as touchy feely, and

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:15.460
so it's a very unstructured ambiguous
class where you're trying to

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:18.259
learn about group dynamics
in the absence of authority.

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:23.420
Structure and say, you think that my
military background wouldn't necessarily

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:26.550
lend itself to that, but I found that
having a lot of negotiations with

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:31.520
tribal chiefs, or
with civilians on deployments actually

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:34.650
gave me an advantage in putting myself out
there a little bit more with that class,

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:36.520
and getting the most
learning possible from it.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:37.820
So, yeah.

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:40.982
&gt;&gt; All right.
Have you encountered any people who

00:20:40.982 --> 00:20:42.222
are hesitant to hire or

00:20:42.222 --> 00:20:45.960
work with veterans because of
your non-traditional background?

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:50.689
I think that the hardest industry I've
seen or heard about to break into here,

00:20:50.689 --> 00:20:52.702
seems to be private equity.

00:20:52.702 --> 00:20:57.600
And that is not a wall
that is impenetrable.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.553
But it's just a more challenging thing
to get into, because mostly tends to be

00:21:00.553 --> 00:21:02.983
people who come from private equity,
that will go back into it.

00:21:02.983 --> 00:21:06.460
But it's doable, but
I haven't seen anyone do it yet.

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:09.010
There's a guy in the class that is.

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:17.750
But otherwise, I think people are here
to hire veterans You are different.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.640
I think that there is a lot of
non-traditional backgrounds that come

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:25.420
here, probably half, and I think
that with the time you have here and

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:28.170
working with career management center and
talking to our peers and classmates that

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:31.440
have been in these industries, you can
figure out how to shape the narrative

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:34.610
that you need to then be able to
sell them on hiring you for the job.

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:38.280
But a lot of it comes down to things
you're good at which is being responsible,

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:42.470
being diligent, being good planner,
getting things done, leading people.

00:21:42.470 --> 00:21:46.600
That's been the message that reinforced to
me time and again since I've been here.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:49.700
&gt;&gt; To build on that,
there are professors here

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:53.230
who are if you're trying to break into
an industry, were better if going into.

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:57.620
You conform relationships with definite
access to giving a shot during your summer

00:21:57.620 --> 00:21:58.600
or in any way.

00:21:58.600 --> 00:22:00.700
So Everything is possible.

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:04.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah and I was actually struck by how
many veterans are in the local area and

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:09.510
that intersection between Tech and
the GSB and all of the different things.

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.590
For example, we just finished
this class called Hacking for

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:16.030
Defense that pit a lot
of different students,

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:20.820
whether it's business background or law
school students or engineering students to

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:24.300
tackle tough problems being faced
by the Department of Defense.

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:26.670
So that's really, really fun.

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:28.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; See what the challenges were.

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:32.500
&gt;&gt; Did you run in to any challenges during
your application process while being in

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:35.350
a military and how did you overcome those?

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:39.410
&gt;&gt; I'd say the biggest challenge was
translating my military experience

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:40.770
into civilian lingo.

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:43.090
Like far and away.

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:46.080
And I never really had to do a resume.

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:48.220
So that was something that
took me a lot of time.

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:49.700
I had to do my research.

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:53.605
I had to figure out how to translate
those things and sell myself.

00:22:53.605 --> 00:22:58.450
And I would definitely give
yourself enough time to do that.

00:22:58.450 --> 00:22:59.350
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
On that,

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:04.030
there's a couple secrets that are helpful
in terms of of doing that effectively,

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:05.510
one is reach out to the Vet's Club.

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:10.130
We have veterans here, you can find
our e-mail, who are always willing and

00:23:10.130 --> 00:23:14.090
helpful with sort of talking through
the process more on the side,

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:16.990
I mean I was helped by a veteran that
was a former graduate of here when I was

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:19.530
trying to think about
my application process.

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:23.160
So, for any school you're thinking about
applying to, reach out to those resources.

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:25.100
They're there available and
waiting for you.

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:27.550
We've all benefited from that and so
people are willing to pay it forward.

00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:34.393
Okay, how do you manage your
time to explore the various

00:23:34.393 --> 00:23:36.458
post MBA options available to you?

00:23:40.916 --> 00:23:44.379
&gt;&gt; That is- [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's hard too, coming up a few weeks

00:23:44.379 --> 00:23:46.930
away from graduation, so,
let me give you my personal example.

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:48.393
I'm going back to the military and

00:23:48.393 --> 00:23:51.986
I still feel stressed about about when I
eventually transition out of the military.

00:23:51.986 --> 00:23:55.785
There are so many post MBA options that
I'm thinking, how do I make sure that I

00:23:55.785 --> 00:23:59.701
have all this sorted out in my head so I
know who to contact when that happens, and

00:23:59.701 --> 00:24:04.608
I can imagine it's probably more stressful
for people who are entering the workforce.

00:24:04.608 --> 00:24:06.730
Right afterwards though,

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:10.910
I think the thing is just to be willing to
be experimental right when you show up and

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:17.454
to have contacts within the industry that
are people you can rely on later on, so.

00:24:17.454 --> 00:24:18.601
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:21.805
Are there any classes?

00:24:21.805 --> 00:24:26.170
Or sorry, I'll do this one, for
an enlisted marine retiring looking for

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:29.360
diverse peer demographic compared
to a veteran heavy MBA program.

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:32.260
Can you speak to the educational and
networking value?

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:33.880
&gt;&gt; I can absolutely do that.

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:37.920
I think one thing I've seen here is
that people are here to learn and

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:41.950
they're here to share their background and
experience with others.

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:47.950
We have people in our class that, you
might be reading a case about the FCC and

00:24:47.950 --> 00:24:51.450
somebody will raise their hand and
say, I was actually

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:55.690
a lawyer working there at the time and
[LAUGH] and this is what really happens.

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:57.230
I mean, it could be anything, and

00:24:57.230 --> 00:25:00.670
somebody's got some unique
experience to bring in there.

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:06.370
That's been fascinating, and there
are worlds I didn't even know existed.

00:25:06.370 --> 00:25:10.980
So I think this gives us the opportunity
to really, really get out there and

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:13.670
see what the world has to offer.

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:18.450
In a way that I worry if I were at
a more veteran heavy organization,

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:20.790
you might fall into some
of those old habits and

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:23.740
patterns where we're kind of racking and
stacking each other.

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:25.650
And those kind of things happen.

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:27.490
Maybe they don't, but
I imagine they could, and

00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:29.620
I don't have to worry about that here.

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:34.200
And I really enjoy just just
learning from my peers, for sure.

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:36.100
&gt;&gt; And I'll reiterate too, the network for

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:41.650
me has been the most valuable aspect
of part of this experience, by far.

00:25:43.190 --> 00:25:47.490
I'm just blown away by classmates and
they have experience, like you said, in so

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:48.530
many different areas.

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:52.300
And there's just the collegiality here and
the willingness to either take a phone or

00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:53.880
to connect you with someone.

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:56.450
It just really broadens the reach
what you can do with this

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:57.690
places at Stanford platform.

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:01.440
&gt;&gt; Totally agree.

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:05.200
&gt;&gt; Okay, have you seen students with
families thrive at the Stanford GSB,

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:08.780
given the competing demands of academic
rigor, social life and family life,

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:10.150
well we have a new father here.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:13.015
And we have a mother as well so
I will sit this one out.

00:26:13.015 --> 00:26:18.835
OK so
I have 11-year old twin boys right now,

00:26:18.835 --> 00:26:23.550
[LAUGH] which I might have
the oldest kids here at the GSB.

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:28.190
And so some of the things that made
me very ordinary when I was in

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:31.390
the military like getting married young,
having kids young and

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:36.030
being a parent Are absolutely
fascinating to others here.

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:38.420
So, they just think I'm just superwoman.

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:42.270
Which I am just paddling along,
trying to do my thing.

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:47.670
And I'm also living off campus so,
that has been quiet the juggling act.

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:52.000
But it is doable and I still feel like
I'm getting that GSBO experience and

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:55.530
And Jacob's got the whole other side of.

00:26:55.530 --> 00:26:59.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
I have a three month old, Samuel.

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:04.970
So for the first three quarters
of my experience here,

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:06.860
I didn't really have to
manage it that much.

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:08.490
Now, being married and

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:13.970
living off campus, like Valerie said,
I'm very close biking distance, but.

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:16.870
It's something you have to
negotiate in terms of how much of

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:18.920
your day do you want to spend at the GSB.

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:21.450
And also, how much do you want
to integrate your spouse or

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:23.810
your partner in the GSB life.

00:27:23.810 --> 00:27:26.120
And I think because I
was just getting settled

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:30.680
I didn't INtegrate my wife as early
as possible into the GSB community.

00:27:30.680 --> 00:27:31.960
But then over time it happened, and

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:34.050
we've realized we had this really
great set of friends here.

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:36.350
And we're going to be very
sad to leave afterwards.

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:40.110
So, I think that answer's going to
be different for everyone, and

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:43.120
you just need to set up
the appropriate boundaries that allow

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:48.040
both sides of your life
to Be really thriving.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:50.380
&gt;&gt; Wow, well,
we're almost out of time today.

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:55.000
Before we go, Valerie and Jake, any final
words of advice for people thinking about

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:59.050
applying for Stanford MBA,
especially if they served in the military?

00:27:59.050 --> 00:28:01.300
&gt;&gt; I'd say don't sell yourself short.

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:02.200
Give it a shot.

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:03.390
You never know what can happen.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:07.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I think that there is value in

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:10.610
experimentation and
reflection like I said earlier.

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:15.645
So taking a couple years of your
life to invest Trust in you, and

00:28:15.645 --> 00:28:18.775
instead of actually rushing
right back into the workforce I

00:28:18.775 --> 00:28:21.795
think it's helpful because you
identify what you're really good at.

00:28:21.795 --> 00:28:24.965
And in a constrained world it's much
better to do what you're really good at

00:28:24.965 --> 00:28:28.985
instead of just trying to overcompensate
for something where you're average.

00:28:28.985 --> 00:28:29.605
So.

00:28:29.605 --> 00:28:30.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:33.940
Yeah, if I've got forty
years left to work,

00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:38.640
I really like the idea of taking
a couple of years to think about and

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:41.700
test out all the different ways I can
go and then move forward from there.

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:45.580
I would also say,
this is like a transition.

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:46.440
It's work.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:47.290
It's a lot of work.

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:49.150
I think when I got out of the military.

00:28:49.150 --> 00:28:52.210
I kind of got out knowing what I was
running from but not necessarily what I

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:56.260
was going to and I had to really develop
and spend some time thinking about that.

00:28:56.260 --> 00:29:02.030
So I think when you're in the lows of,
this is taking a lot of my time,

00:29:02.030 --> 00:29:04.860
how can it possibly be so hard to think so
much about these things and

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:09.790
this process is incredibly complex
I would say that resoundingly it's

00:29:09.790 --> 00:29:14.250
been worth it to get through that and to
keep pushing and to continue exploring and

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:17.270
it's, you know, there just a lot
of effort that goes into it but

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:20.110
there's a lot of upside
on the back end of that.

00:29:20.110 --> 00:29:20.730
So keep pushing.

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:27.690
Great, well thank you both for
joining us and for

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:29.870
everyone in the audience for
participating.

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:33.090
You can learn more about the MBA
program on the GSB website.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:36.990
And the application for the class starting
in the fall of 2017 will be available

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:38.560
in June, so
you've got a little bit of time.

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:41.380
If you have questions
about the MBA program,

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:43.730
you can continue to submit
them in the chat box.

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:47.590
Staff from the MBA Admissions Office will
answer them for the next 15 minutes.

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:49.420
Thanks again and have a great day.

